  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has `timestamp` as its time field. The response code is the `response` field. It is text, so I used its keyword subfield `response.keyword` for aggregation and filtering.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-ad0c23d3 Web logs' has exactly two panels, both on that data view. One is a line chart of requests over time. The other is a bar chart of requests by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Rule:** 'S4-ad0c23d3 5xx errors' is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests have `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\" within 5 minutes. The range works as a string comparison because every code has three digits. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. I didn't attach any actions, since none were requested.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
